      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Supravalvar aortic stenosis (SVAS) is a congenital anomaly characterized by a discrete or diffuse narrowing of the ascending aorta. It may also be associated with right-ventricular outflow tract obstruction, aortic valve pathology and coronary ostial stenosis. While present in both familial and sporadic forms, it demonstrates a strong association with William-Beuren syndrome, both being anomalies associated with defects in the elastin gene. In this article, the authors have discussed the etiology, morphology, clinical presentation and genetic basis of SVAS. Various surgical approaches, both conventional and recent, have been discussed and demonstrated with the aid of diagrams. Single-, two- and three-sinus methods have been presented, along with a comparative analysis of early results, associated procedures, late mortality and reoperation. In conclusion, the authors have described their institutional experience of more than 40 years in the surgical management of SVAS."},
